# Intel GPU support — covers ~everything from Eaglelake (G41, GMA X4500)
# through current Iris Xe. The `i915` kernel module is in `linux-image`
# by default; this profile fills in userspace + firmware so the GPU is
# fully functional, not just framebuffer-only.
#
# `xserver-xorg-video-intel` is the legacy DDX driver — many users have
# moved to the kernel `modesetting` driver in xserver-xorg-core, but for
# older chipsets (G41, GMA X4500, Ironlake) the intel DDX still gives
# better behaviour than modesetting.
xserver-xorg-video-intel
# Pre-modern Intel chipsets benefit from VESA/fbdev fallbacks too —
# cheap to ship, useful when modesetting flakes.
xserver-xorg-video-vesa
xserver-xorg-video-fbdev
# Mesa userspace — GL + DRI. No Vulkan: mesa-vulkan-drivers pulls in
# libllvm19 (126 MB) and itself takes 81 MB, and the typical Intel
# fleet target (older OptiPlex-class hardware through current Iris
# Xe) either lacks Vulkan entirely (G41 / GMA X4500 / Ironlake) or
# doesn't depend on it for desktop rendering. Admins running modern
# GPU-accelerated workloads can re-add mesa-vulkan-drivers via
# image/custom/extra-packages.list per-deployment.
mesa-utils
libgl1-mesa-dri
# CPU microcode updates. Small (~50MB) and useful for any Intel CPU.
intel-microcode
# Note: firmware-misc-nonfree (~700MB!) is a catch-all containing
# firmware for hundreds of devices most fleets don't have. We
# deliberately don't include it — admins targeting hardware that
# needs it (some recent Intel Wi-Fi cards, certain network adapters)
# can add it via image/custom/extra-packages.list per-deployment.
